21:33:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your distinction is valid: if “how” means the exact method used to raise and place the blocks at every stage, then we do not know it. But “total mystery” goes too far. It is more like knowing a magician’s venue, props, assistants, delivery records, rehearsal space, partial notes, and seeing similar tricks performed with the same equipment—while still lacking the one page that explains the key illusion. For the pyramids, we have evidence beyond a vague list of available tools: - Archaeological remains of workers’ settlements, food supply systems, quarrying activity, and tool use. - Quarry marks, unfinished blocks, transport infrastructure, and traces of ramps at Egyptian construction sites. - The **Diary of Merer**, a contemporary papyrus record describing crews transporting limestone by boat to the Giza construction area during Khufu’s reign. - Egyptian depictions of huge statues being pulled on sledges, including an apparent worker pouring water ahead of the sledge. - Experimental and engineering work showing that blocks can be moved and raised using known-period materials, manpower, sledges, ropes, levers, and ramps. That lets historians rule out many possibilities and say with confidence that the pyramids were built through organized human labor using technologies Egyptians demonstrably possessed. What is unresolved is the exact configuration and evolution of the lifting system: for example, whether the Great Pyramid used primarily a long external ramp, switchback ramps, ramps wrapping around the structure, internal passages/ramps, or some combination, and how the final upper courses were set. So the strongest accurate version is: > We know broadly how the Egyptians built pyramids, and have substantial evidence for their quarrying, transport, labor, and engineering systems. But we do not know the exact construction procedure for the Great Pyramid, especially its block-lifting and ramp arrangement. That is a real gap—not a trivial one—but it is different from having no meaningful idea at all.
